%N A400379 Number of nonempty sets of pairwise commuting permutations of [n] generating a transitive group.
%C A400379 If X is counted by a(n), then <X> is Abelian and transitive. Every transitive Abelian permutation group is regular, hence has order n.
%C A400379 For an Abelian group A of order n, the number of regular subgroups of S_n isomorphic to A is (n-1)!/|Aut(A)|.
%H A400379 Vladeta Jovovic, <a href="/A400379/b400379.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..30</a>
%F A400379 Let A run through the isomorphism classes of Abelian groups of order n, and let g(A) be the number of subsets of A which generate A.
%F A400379 Then a(n) = (n-1)! * Sum_A g(A)/|Aut(A)|, where g(A) = Sum_{H <= A} mu_A(H,A)*2^|H|, and mu_A is the Mobius function of the subgroup lattice of A.
%F A400379 If n > 1 is squarefree, then a(n) = ((n-1)!/phi(n)) * Sum_{d | n} mu(d)*2^(n/d).
%F A400379 In particular, for prime p, a(p) = (p-2)!*(2^p - 2).
%e A400379 For n = 4, the Abelian groups are C_4 and C_2^2. We have g(C_4) = 12, |Aut(C_4)| = 2, g(C_2^2) = 8, and |Aut(C_2^2)| = 6.
%e A400379 Hence a(4) = 3!*(12/2 + 8/6) = 44.
%Y A400379 Cf. A399972 (nonempty sets of pairwise commuting permutations of [n]), A000688 (Abelian groups of order n), A058162 (labeled Abelian groups with a fixed identity), A034382 (labeled Abelian groups of order n).
